   "format": "mcq",
   "stem": {
      "text": "The Maclaurin series \\( \\cos x = \\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty} \\frac{(-1)^{n} x^{2n}}{(2n)!} \\) is evaluated at \\( x = \\frac{1}{2} \\). The sum of its first three nonzero terms is used to approximate \\( \\cos\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\). Find the alternating series error bound for this approximation."
   },
   "answer_key": {
      "form": "symbolic",
      "mathjson": [
         "Rational",
         1,
         46080
      ]
   },
   "worked_solution": [
      {
         "step": 1,
         "text": "At \\( x = \\frac{1}{2} \\) the series alternates, and its terms \\( \\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})^{m}}{m!} \\) decrease in absolute value to 0.",
         "mathjson": [
            "Rational",
            1,
            2
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 2,
         "text": "The first three nonzero terms stop at the power \\( x^{4} \\), so the first omitted term has power \\( x^{6} \\).",
         "mathjson": 6
      },
      {
         "step": 3,
         "text": "Its absolute value at \\( x = \\frac{1}{2} \\) is \\( \\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})^{6}}{6!} = \\frac{1}{46080} \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Rational",
            1,
            46080
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 4,
         "text": "The error is at most \\( \\frac{1}{46080} \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Rational",
            1,
            46080
         ]
      }
   ],
